---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Num from "../components/Num.astro";
import { generation, vocab, n, corpusStats, corpusSources, sourceShare,
         leaderboard, POPULARITY, href, stability } from "../lib/data";
import { frame, scaleLinear, scaleBand, ticks, fPct1 } from "../lib/chart";

const top20 = vocab.by_frequency.slice(0, 20);
const share = (c: number) => c / generation.slots;
const top20Share = top20.reduce((s, v) => s + share(v.count), 0);

/* Top-20 bars. The previous version sized bars as a percentage of salt, which
   made salt a full-width bar and left no way to read an actual value off the
   chart. A real x axis in percentage points fixes that. */
const barF = frame(660, 20 * 21 + 34, { top: 6, right: 46, bottom: 28, left: 112 });
const barX = scaleLinear()
  .domain([0, share(top20[0].count)])
  .nice()
  .range([barF.x0, barF.x1]);
const barY = scaleBand<string>()
  .domain(top20.map((v) => v.name))
  .range([barF.y0, barF.y1])
  .padding(0.28);
const barTicks = ticks(barX, 5, fPct1);
const tail = vocab.by_frequency.filter((v) => share(v.count) < 0.0001).length;

// Twenty-nine rows is a data dump; the shape of the imbalance is carried by
// the handful of sources that are actually large. The rest are summed rather
// than dropped, so the column still totals to the corpus.
const w = corpusStats?.waterfall
  ?? { scanned: 0, kept: 0, dropped_duplicate: 0,
       dropped_matched_nothing: 0, slots: 0 };
const shown = corpusSources.filter((r) => sourceShare(r.kept) >= 0.01);
const rest = corpusSources.filter((r) => sourceShare(r.kept) < 0.01);
const restScanned = rest.reduce((s, r) => s + r.scanned, 0);
const restKept = rest.reduce((s, r) => s + r.kept, 0);
---

<Base
  title="What this does not show"
  chapter="Eight — limits"
  standfirst="The strongest version of this result is narrower than the headline. Here is where it stops."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>The vocabulary is a ceiling, not a description</h2>
    <p>
      {n(vocab.n)} ingredients is a normalised vocabulary, not the set of
      things people cook with. Every brand, every cut, every regional variant
      collapses into a canonical term or is dropped. A model that cannot
      distinguish two things the vocabulary already merged is not being
      measured on that distinction.
    </p>
    <p>
      The distribution is also extremely skewed. The top twenty ingredients
      account for <Num value={top20Share} pct dp={1} tone="accent" /> of all{" "}
      {n(generation.slots)} ingredient slots, and {n(tail)} of{" "}
      {n(vocab.n)} ingredients appear in under one slot in ten thousand.
      That skew is why the popularity baseline is strong, and it means the
      result is partly a statement about <i>this</i> distribution rather than
      about embeddings in general.
    </p>
  </div>

  <figure class="topfig wide-figure">
    <figcaption class="label">Share of all ingredient slots, top 20</figcaption>
    <svg class="topbars" viewBox={barF.viewBox} role="img"
         aria-label={`Bar chart. The twenty most common ingredients, led by ${top20[0].name.replace(/_/g, " ")} at ${(share(top20[0].count) * 100).toFixed(2)} percent of all ingredient slots.`}>
      {barTicks.map((t) => (
        <g>
          <line class="grid" x1={t.pos} x2={t.pos} y1={barF.y0} y2={barF.y1} />
          <text class="tick" x={t.pos} y={barF.y1 + 16} text-anchor="middle">{t.label}</text>
        </g>
      ))}
      <line class="axis" x1={barF.x0} x2={barF.x0} y1={barF.y0} y2={barF.y1} />
      {top20.map((v) => (
        <g>
          <text class="nm" x={barF.x0 - 8} y={(barY(v.name) ?? 0) + barY.bandwidth() / 2}
                text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central">{v.name.replace(/_/g, " ")}</text>
          <rect class="bar" x={barF.x0} y={barY(v.name)}
                width={barX(share(v.count)) - barF.x0} height={barY.bandwidth()} />
          <text class="val" x={barX(share(v.count)) + 6}
                y={(barY(v.name) ?? 0) + barY.bandwidth() / 2}
                dominant-baseline="central">{(share(v.count) * 100).toFixed(2)}</text>
        </g>
      ))}
    </svg>
  </figure>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>The corpus is Western and English-dominated</h2>
    <p>
      The corpus is assembled from 29 sources, and it is not balanced across
      them — one large English-language recipe collection dominates. Cuisines
      whose ingredient co-occurrence structure differs from that majority are
      under-represented, and a model's failure on them would not show up in
      any number on this site.
    </p>
    {corpusStats ? (
      <>
        <p>
          The concentration is not a detail. The two largest sources are{" "}
          <Num value={sourceShare(corpusSources[0].kept)
                 + sourceShare(corpusSources[1].kept)} pct dp={1} /> of every
          recipe the models ever saw, and the largest alone is{" "}
          <Num value={sourceShare(corpusSources[0].kept)} pct dp={1} />.
          Twenty-seven sources share the remainder.
        </p>

        <figure class="wide-figure">
          <table class="survival">
            <caption>
              Every recipe the readers produced, and what became of it.
              Counted in the pass that builds the corpus, so the table cannot
              disagree with the artefact. Sources below 1% are grouped.
            </caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">source</th>
                <th scope="col" class="num">scanned</th>
                <th scope="col" class="num">kept</th>
                <th scope="col" class="num">survival</th>
                <th scope="col" class="num">share of corpus</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {shown.map((r) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{r.source}</td>
                  <td class="num">{n(r.scanned)}</td>
                  <td class="num">{n(r.kept)}</td>
                  <td class="num"><Num value={r.kept / r.scanned} pct dp={1} /></td>
                  <td class="num">
                    <span class="bar" style={`--w:${sourceShare(r.kept) * 100}%`}
                          aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    <Num value={sourceShare(r.kept)} pct dp={1} />
                  </td>
                </tr>
              ))}
              {rest.length > 0 && (
                <tr class="grouped">
                  <td>{rest.length} smaller sources</td>
                  <td class="num">{n(restScanned)}</td>
                  <td class="num">{n(restKept)}</td>
                  <td class="num"><Num value={restKept / restScanned} pct dp={1} /></td>
                  <td class="num"><Num value={sourceShare(restKept)} pct dp={1} /></td>
                </tr>
              )}
            </tbody>
            <tfoot>
              <tr>
                <td>all {corpusStats.n_sources} sources</td>
                <td class="num">{n(w.scanned)}</td>
                <td class="num">{n(w.kept)}</td>
                <td class="num"><Num value={w.kept / w.scanned} pct dp={1} /></td>
                <td class="num">100%</td>
              </tr>
            </tfoot>
          </table>
        </figure>

        <p>
          {n(w.scanned - w.kept)} recipes
          (<Num value={(w.scanned - w.kept) / w.scanned} pct dp={2} />) were
          dropped on the way in: {n(w.dropped_duplicate)} were exact
          duplicates of a recipe already read, and
          {" "}{n(w.dropped_matched_nothing)} matched no vocabulary ingredient
          at all. Deduplication is by
          ingredient set, so a recipe reprinted across two sites is counted
          once — which is also why the surviving share of a source says as
          much about its overlap with the others as about its quality.
        </p>
      </>
    ) : (
      <p class="muted">
        The per-source survival table is generated by{" "}
        <code>scripts/rebuild_corpus.py --stats-only</code> and is not yet
        included in this build.
      </p>
    )}
    <p>
      Eight of the twenty-nine sources have no text reader, so their rows in
      the text index are aligned but blank. That affects the text-conditioned
      models specifically, and those models are not the ones the headline
      depends on.
    </p>

    <h2>Nothing here measures whether the food is good</h2>
    <p>
      This is the most important limit and the easiest to forget. The task is{" "}
      <b>reconstructing an ingredient that a human already chose</b>. A model
      that scores well has learned what people actually cook, not what tastes
      good, and certainly not what would taste good in a combination nobody
      has tried.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is no human judgment anywhere in this evaluation. A suggestion
      that is novel and excellent is scored identically to one that is novel
      and inedible: both are wrong, because neither is the ingredient that was
      hidden. Any use of these models for <i>discovery</i> rather than
      completion is unmeasured by everything on this site.
    </p>

    <h2>One split, one task, one draw</h2>
    <ul class="lims">
      <li>
        <b>One task.</b> Recall@10 on single-ingredient completion. Models
        might order differently on ingredient substitution, quantity
        prediction, or full-recipe generation — none of which is tested here.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>One split.</b> <code>recipe-holdout</code>, 30% of recipes. It is
        the honest split for this comparison, but it is one split.
      </li>
      <li>
        {stability ? (
          <>
            <b>{stability.seeds.length} seeds, one split.</b> Every model was
            retrained at {stability.seeds.length} seeds, and no model changed
            which side of the baseline it fell on. The ordering is less solid
            than the verdict: {stability.n_models_changing_rank} of{" "}
            {stability.n_models_complete} models swap adjacent positions
            between seeds, so neighbouring ranks should not be read as
            separating two models. Every score quoted elsewhere on this site
            is seed 0.{" "}
            <a href={href("robustness/")}>The sweep is here</a>.
          </>
        ) : (
          <>
            <b>One seed.</b> Every published number is seed 0, so the ordering
            should be treated as provisional.
          </>
        )}
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>No hyperparameter search.</b> Each model was run at reasonable
        defaults. A tuned version of a below-baseline model might clear the
        line, and the claim is about the methods as commonly applied, not
        about their best achievable performance.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What survives all of that</h2>
    <p>
      Narrowed as far as it will go, the result still stands:{" "}
      <b>on this corpus, on this task, at reasonable defaults,{" "}
      {leaderboard.n_below_popularity} of {leaderboard.n_models} standard
      embedding methods score below{" "}
      <Num value={POPULARITY} tone="accent" />, which is what you get by
      counting.</b> That is a small claim. It is also one that almost nobody
      checks before shipping.
    </p>
    <p class="next"><a href={href("/methods/")}>Continue: methods and reproduction →</a></p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  code { background: var(--paper-sunk); padding: 0 0.25em; }
  .topfig { margin-block: var(--s-6) var(--s-7); }
  figcaption { color: var(--ink-faint); margin-bottom: var(--s-4); }
  .topbars { width: 100%; height: auto; overflow: visible; }
  .topbars .grid { stroke: var(--rule); stroke-width: 1; }
  .topbars .axis { stroke: var(--ink); stroke-width: 1; }
  .topbars .bar { fill: var(--accent); }
  .topbars .nm { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 12px; fill: var(--ink-soft); }
  .topbars .val { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 11px;
                  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; fill: var(--ink-faint); }
  .topbars .tick { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 11px; fill: var(--ink-faint); }

  .lims { padding-left: 1.1rem; }
  .lims li { margin-bottom: var(--s-3); }
  .next { margin-top: var(--s-6); font-size: var(--t-md); }

  /* Same table language as the leaderboard: dense, ruled, tabular figures,
   * decimal-aligned. A second visual idiom here would read as a different
   * document. */
  .wide-figure { margin-block: var(--s-6); overflow-x: auto; }
  .survival { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse;
              font-family: var(--sans); font-size: var(--t-sm); }
  .survival caption { caption-side: top; text-align: left;
                      padding-bottom: var(--s-3); color: var(--ink-faint);
                      max-width: var(--measure); }
  .survival thead th { text-align: left; font-weight: 600;
                       font-size: var(--t-xs); letter-spacing: 0.07em;
                       text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-faint);
                       padding: 0 var(--s-3) var(--s-2) 0;
                       border-bottom: 1.5px solid var(--ink-soft);
                       white-space: nowrap; }
  .survival thead th.num { text-align: right; }
  .survival tbody td { padding: 0.42rem var(--s-3) 0.42rem 0;
                       border-bottom: 1px solid var(--paper-edge);
                       vertical-align: baseline; }
  .survival td.num { text-align: right; white-space: nowrap;
                     font-family: var(--mono);
                     font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums lining-nums;
                     font-feature-settings: "tnum" 1, "lnum" 1;
                     letter-spacing: -0.012em; }
  .survival tbody tr:hover { background: var(--paper-sunk); }
  .survival .grouped td { color: var(--ink-faint); font-style: italic; }
  .survival tfoot td { padding: var(--s-2) var(--s-3) 0 0;
                       border-top: 1.5px solid var(--ink-soft);
                       font-weight: 600; }
  .survival tfoot td.num { text-align: right; }

  /* The bar sits behind its own number rather than in a separate column, so
   * the eye gets the magnitude and the value in one place. */
  .survival .bar { display: inline-block; height: 0.5rem; width: var(--w);
                   background: var(--accent); opacity: 0.35;
                   margin-right: var(--s-2); vertical-align: middle;
                   max-width: 6rem; }
</style>
